From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 04:02:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkvdec: vdpu381: adapt the VP9 probabilities in
 hardware

The VP9 back-end of the VDPU381 applies the forward probability updates
of the compressed header and the backward adaptation (spec 8.4) on the
CPU: rkvdec_vp9_done() reads the symbol counts of a frame back and
adapts the frame context before the next frame can be set up. That
keeps a stream on one core, one frame at a time.

The VDPU381 can do both itself, which is how the Rockchip MPP drives it
(the HW_PROB path of hal_vp9d_vdpu34x_gen_regs() in
hal_vp9d_vdpu34x.c): the driver passes the updates of the compressed
header, the hardware reads the probabilities of the frame context, adapts
them to the frame and saves them back. Do the same:

 - Every job gets an update table: a bit per probability telling whether
   the compressed header updates it, then the updates, for intra frames
   with the key frame partition and intra mode probabilities. V4L2 passes
   the updates translated through inv_map_table[] (spec 6.3.5), the
   hardware wants the coded index, so translate them back; motion vector
   updates are new probabilities and are passed as they are. The layouts
   are those of hal_vp9d_prob_flag_delta() in MPP's hal_vp9d_com.c, the
   values those its VP9 parser derives from the compressed header
   (vp9d_parser.c, vp9d_diff_update_prob() in vp9d_codec.c).
 - Every stream gets four frame context buffers, which only the hardware
   writes (prob_save_en, refresh_en for the backward adaptation, as in
   6.1.2 refresh_probs()), and a table of the default probabilities
   (hal_vp9d_prob_default()) that a frame reads instead of a frame context
   that was reset and not saved since. The buffers are told apart by their
   index + 1 in reg028 (vp9_rd_prob_idx/vp9_wr_prob_idx).
 - Every job gets its own symbol count buffer, which the hardware writes.
 - The state the next frame needs from the previous one is updated when
   the frame is set up, not when it is done.

The frame context resets follow setup_past_independence() of the spec
rather than MPP, which drops all frame contexts on every intra frame: an
intra-only frame with reset_frame_context 0 or 1 keeps them and uses
frame context 0. The coefficient update factor flag (spec 8.4.3) is set
after a key frame, MPP sets it after any intra frame.

The hardware covers every case the CPU adaptation handled, so drop the
CPU path: the adaptation code, the symbol count layouts and the CPU
copies of the frame contexts.

The update and default tables were checked on the host against MPP's
hal_vp9d_prob_flag_delta() and hal_vp9d_prob_default(), byte for byte,
for 4000 random compressed headers of intra and inter frames.

